    "noise_floor_fraction": 0.5164,
    "notes": [
      "Displacement is measured along the radar line of sight and is relative: the median of the usable pixels in the window (+25.1 cm before referencing) was set to zero. Positive values mean the ground moved toward the satellite (for example uplift); negative values mean it moved away from the satellite (for example subsidence).",
      "Empirical noise level: the robust spread of the motion field (1.4826 x median absolute deviation) is 13.62 cm, and 10.07 to 14.14 cm across the processing choices.",
      "A single 12-day pair typically carries about 1 to 2 cm of atmospheric noise, so smaller signals cannot be trusted. The noise level is therefore never taken below 1 cm.",
      "Decision rule: motion is reported only if the 1st-to-99th percentile range exceeds 9 x the noise level (22.5 cm here) for every processing choice and the field still stands out after a planar ramp is removed (same test, or a spread above 5 cm). Ideal noise gives 4.65 x; 44 real NISAR pairs without an earthquake gave 2.6 to 6.9 x. Smallest range found: 54.2 cm; after ramp removal: 46.8 cm.",
      "52% of the window was masked: 12% coherence below 0.3, 36% water, 4% not reliably unwrapped, 0% no data.",
      "The field's spread (13.6 cm) is far larger than atmosphere can explain, so it is attributed to motion and the noise level is set to 2.5 cm, the largest value this workflow accepts as atmosphere, rather than measured. Scatter over short distances (2.6 km tiles) is 0.94 cm.",
      "Ionosphere: NASA's ionosphere phase screen spans 12.8 cm across the usable area. NASA ships it as a separate layer and does not apply it; here it WAS subtracted. Without it the robust spread would be 13.17 cm instead of 13.62 cm. NASA's own uncertainty for that estimate is 3.1 cm (median).",
      "Planar ramp: a best-fit plane changes by -28.1 cm from west to east and +33.9 cm from south to north across the window. It was kept (a ramp can be real motion).",
      "Noise from loss of coherence alone is about 0.18 cm per pixel (median coherence 0.467, 208 looks): the noise here is mostly atmospheric, not instrumental."
    ]
  },
  "caveats": [
    {
      "code": "line_of_sight",
      "text": "This is motion along the radar's slanted line of sight, not vertical motion and not full 3-D motion. Horizontal and vertical movement are mixed together. The radar looked down at 41 degrees from vertical with the satellite to the east-north-east: one centimetre here is 0.75 x upward + +0.65 x eastward + +0.14 x northward motion."
    },
    {
      "code": "relative_motion",
      "text": "Values are relative to the median of the usable pixels in the drawn area, not to a stable reference point. If the whole area moved together, that shared motion is invisible here."
    },
    {
      "code": "atmosphere",
      "text": "Water vapour in the atmosphere delays the radar signal differently on the two dates and typically adds 1 to 2 cm of smooth, false motion to a single pair. No atmospheric (troposphere) correction was applied."
    },
    {
      "code": "ionosphere",
      "text": "NISAR's L-band radar is strongly affected by the ionosphere. NASA's ionosphere estimate was subtracted, but that estimate has its own error and is interpolated across gaps."
    },
    {
      "code": "unwrapping",
      "text": "Turning fringes into continuous motion (phase unwrapping) can slip by whole fringes of 12.1 cm across gaps of low coherence such as water, forest, steep slopes or a fault rupture. Sharp steps of about that size should be treated as suspect."
    },
    {
      "code": "tier_provisional",
      "text": "This pair is provisional-tier: calibrated, but values may change after NASA reprocesses it."
    },
    {
      "code": "single_pair",
      "text": "The pair spans only 12 days (13 June 2026 to 25 June 2026). It shows what moved between those two passes and cannot show slow motion, timing within the interval, or whether motion continued afterwards."
    }
  ],
